it's been two years since some of  india's most  recognizable rights activists were  rounded up and taken away by police  they're accused of instigating violence  during a march by disadvantaged hindus  in maharashtra state where one person  was killed  eighty-year-old poet vadavadarao and  prominent human rights lawyer sudha  bharadwaj  are among the detained their arrests led  to nationwide protests by their  supporters  most of the 11 accused are yet to be  formally charged  in delhi sudha bharadwaja's daughter  maisha reads a letter from her mother  sent from jail in mumbai what is this  this is not fair and it's not a joke  right  this is her life this is my life and  they're ruined  they are ruining our life  human rights lawyer shalom has worked  with sudha bharadwaj for 10 years  she says only one of the 11 accused was  at the march  we feel right from the beginning it was  a very politically motivated case  as far as a criminal case goes there is  no evidence it is really that  politically it was thought that these  people need to be punished for the work  they're doing  all of them are very well known very  well reputed human rights activists  no stranger to prisons having previously  spent 10 years in jail for inciting  communist revolutionaries  but he has never been found guilty his  family are calling for his immediate  release  after he contracted coronavirus in  prison  we are very much worried about his  health and his  life this is a question of life and  death  because he is 18.  critics say the government is using  anti-terrorism legislation which allows  detention without charge  or trial human rights watch is among  many groups  asking the government why it hasn't  investigated the alleged role  of hindu nationalists in the violence  that rao and baradwaj are in jail for  even though a fact-finding mission found  hindu nationalist leaders  were responsible no justice the bjp  government insists there's no political  interference in the case  because the accusation is that the bjp  government protects  it it doesn't matter the government  doesn't come into the picture  it'd be 100 people yeah in a democracy  because 100 people are saying something  that doesn't become the truth  because there are a thousand people who  say no they were not involved  myself advantage is waiting for her  mother's next bail hearing  she's been joined by the united nations  and european parliament  who are calling for the release of human  rights defenders  elizabeth piranham al jazeera new delhi
 

